[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Everyone agrees on one thing about lithium ion batteries. They\'re brilliant for about 4 hours of energy storage or less. What? That wasn\'t what you were going to agree on? Anyway, past that the price stops making sense. So, China went and built out an alternative. Out of thin air. In March'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '2026, they switched on the largest compressed air battery ever built. It sits in a salt cavern under the city of Huai\'an in Jiangsu province in eastern China. When power is cheap, [music] giant compressors stuff air down into'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that cavern and save the heat they make on the way in. When the grid wants it [music] back, the heat and the air go back through a turbine together. No lithium, no fuel burned to get the power back out. And 71% of the electricity that you put in [music] comes back out,'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'which for this kind of system is a very big deal. Now, the real question is, does it fix the thing it\'s supposed to fix? Now, I did the math. [music] Think of it like a water tank. Megawatt hours are how much is in the tank. Megawatts'

[00:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'are how fast it pours. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; This one holds 2.4 gigawatt hours and pours at 600 megawatts. Divide and you get how long the tap runs. 4 hours. That\'s it. The whole pitch for'

[01:02] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'compressed air is that [music] it beats lithium when you need extra long duration energy storage, like 8 hours or all night. And the biggest one ever built runs for four, which is exactly where lithium already wins. And before the comments beat me to it, largest'

[01:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] here means power rating, how hard it can pour, not how much it holds. The McIntosh plant in Alabama has beaten it since 1991. So, what\'s going on here? &gt;&gt; [music]'

[01:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Did China just build the equivalent of a very expensive gas peaker plant that runs on air? And if the economics are as good as the industry [music] claims, why hasn\'t anyone in the West finished one? I told you 2 years ago that compressed air was finally here. But, is it really? I\'m Matt Ferrell. Welcome to Undecided.'

[01:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; This video is brought to you by MicroCenter. When do you think your house actually uses [music] the most power? Well, it\'s after the sun goes down. Everybody\'s home, the oven\'s on, the TV\'s going, and'

[01:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the car\'s plugged in for the night. Solar handles none of that at night. So, every bit of solar power you use in the evening has to come out of storage first. Those 4 hours of storage covered dinner. They don\'t cover a cloudy week'

[02:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in January, and they don\'t cover the night when winter storms drop snow all over your panels. That\'s the gap long-duration storage is supposed to fill, and it\'s the reason that anyone bothers with a 71% efficient system when a lithium battery gives you 85 or 90%'

[02:18] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'round-trip efficiency. So, what are you actually buying here? If you want another hour, buy another rack of cells. Want a third hour? Buy another rack. Every hour costs about what the first one did, somewhere around 300 to 350 dollars per kilowatt-hour.'

[02:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'And that line keeps climbing forever. It\'s like being told the only way to get a bigger gas tank is to buy a second car. Compressed air splits that bill in two. You buy the pump separately from the tank. There\'s the machinery, which'

[02:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is the compressors and the turbines, which runs about a thousand dollars per kilowatt. And then there\'s the hole in the ground, which is the cavern that the air actually sits in. Once you\'ve paid for the machinery, making the hole bigger costs about 20 dollars per kilowatt-hour.'

[02:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '20 dollars against 300 dollars of lithium battery storage. It\'s about 7 cents on the dollar. Hold on to that because it\'s the entire argument behind every project in this video. It also came from Curtis Van Walleghem, the CEO'

[03:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of Canadian company Hydrostor, who sells compressed air storage for a living. And the math is mathing, but it\'s good to validate someone\'s claims, especially if they\'re selling you a product. Now, quick refresher because the number'

[03:22] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'only means [music] something when we look at the big picture. Compressed air storage is an old idea. The first plant opened at Huntorf in northern Germany in 1978, and the McIntosh plant in Alabama followed in 1991. And both still run. The concept is simple. When power is'

[03:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cheap, you run a compressor and cram air into an underground cavern. When power is expensive, you let the air back out through a turbine to generate electricity. The problem is heat. Squeeze air and it gets hot. Let it expand and then gets cold, which is why'

[03:49] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a can of compressed air gets cold and [music] frost over when you spray it. Huntorf and McIntosh both vented the compression heat straight into the sky, then had to burn natural gas to warm the air back up on the way back out. It\'s a'

[04:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'bit like running a hot bath, pulling the plug, and then boiling a kettle to fill it again. Huntorf gets about 42% round trip efficiency doing that. More than half the energy you put in [music] never comes back, and you\'re burning gas in a'

[04:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'machine that\'s supposed to be replacing gas. McIntosh is rated for 26 hours, longer than anything else in this video, and burning gas [music] is how they get there. Which is the whole reason the next number matters. Adiabatic systems'

[04:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'fix [music] that heat leak by keeping the heat instead of throwing it away. China\'s Wyang plant catches it in a molten salt and pressurized hot water, &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; parks it there, then it shoves it back into the air stream before the turbine.'

[04:37] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Think thermos instead of open mug. No kettle, no gas, and round trip efficiency goes from 42 [music] to 71%. That jump is the actual news here. It took about 50 years to make it, and China made it at a scale that nobody'

[04:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'else has attempted. &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; And it\'s bigger than it looks because Huntorf\'s 42% only counts electricity in against electricity out. It ignores the gas. And one caveat before we go further, the 71% comes from the vendors.'

[05:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Harbin Electric and Shanghai Electric both put out releases claiming credit for the equipment, and both quote roughly 71%. There\'s no independent operating data yet, and I\'d love to see'

[05:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a year of real numbers from somebody who isn\'t selling turbines. Compressed air isn\'t the only weird and unique way to hold [music] a day\'s worth of electricity. Let\'s talk about the CO2 battery because the Milan-based company'

[05:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Energy Dome has had a very good few years since I last talked about them. Their system works on the same basic idea with a twist. Instead of air, it uses carbon dioxide in a sealed loop. &gt;&gt; [music]'

[05:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Compress the CO2 until it turns liquid, store the heat separately, then reverse it. The The lives in a big fabric dome that inflates and deflates, so nothing vents and nothing gets used up. Picture a bouncy castle the size [music] of a'

[05:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'city block breathing in and out once a day. That dome is the tank sitting right there at the surface where you can watch it work. When I covered this in 2022, it was a 4 megawatt hour pilot project in Oristano, Sardinia with a long way to'

[06:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'go. Now Google\'s buying it. They\'re signing a 10-year deal with a balloon. Now I\'ll get to why in a second, but first, today\'s sponsor, Micro Center. A quick confession, I\'m an Apple guy, but I\'ve been building my own PCs for'

[06:16] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'decades, and I still can\'t quit. My latest is a tiny gaming rig running Linux for the living room hooked up to my big screen [music] for my Steam library. My Mac Studio is the brain of my smart home. It runs Home Assistant, handles my automations, and powers my'

[06:28] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'own self-hosted AI voice assistants. All local, all mine, none of it leaves the house. And for years, whenever I needed a part fast and affordable, Micro Center was my move. I practically lived at the Cambridge store, so I\'m happy they\'re'

[06:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'sponsoring this one. September is build, upgrade, [music] save month over there, which is basically a holiday for people like me. They have some genuinely great deals such as the Ryzen CPU and motherboard bundles. That\'s the cheapest way I know to start a home server build. They\'re'

[06:53] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'running clearance all month, too, and that\'s where I\'d go for hunting for storage like a Crucial portable SSD. My video editing setup eats drives for breakfast. Now one store update as well, Austin, Texas, you\'re finally getting a Micro Center.'

[07:06] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; Sign up with the link below for a free 128 gig flash drive and grab it when the doors open. And for everything else tech, check out the Micro Center news. All the links in the description, and thanks to Micro Center and to all of you for supporting the channel. Okay, back to that balloon. Here\'s what Google'

[07:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'actually signed up for. Google signed on as the sole customer for a CO2 battery in County Offaly, Ireland. 23 megawatts, 200 megawatt hours with land, planning permission, a grid connection to the Dublin network, and a 10-year capacity'

[07:31] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'contract from the Irish grid operator, EirGrid, already locked down. It should be running in 2028, and Energy Dome plans to put a second one right next to it. There\'s another dome going up in Wisconsin with the utility Alliant'

[07:44] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Energy, 20 MW and 200 MW hours, and a third with Salt River Project in Arizona. Run the same division on those and Ireland comes out at 8.7 hours on paper, though Energy Dome\'s own COO calls it an 8-hour system in practice. Wisconsin is'

[07:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a clean 10. Those are long-duration machines. They sit exactly where the cheap energy storage hours argument says they should. [music] In Energy Dome CEO Claudio Spedicato\'s public claim is careful in a way that I appreciate. He'

[08:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'says that past 8 hours, the CO2 battery is cost-competitive with lithium. Past 8 hours. That qualifier is the whole ballgame, and I respect him for leaving it in. Energy Dome won\'t publish a levelized cost of its own, but the'

[08:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'number floating around in a lot of coverage is about 11 cents per kilowatt-hour, which comes from an academic paper modeling a compressed CO2 system, not from an Energy Dome plant. So, hold the domes up against our'

[08:35] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'question. On duration, they\'re the best answer anyone has. On everything else, they\'re tiny. Ireland is 23 MW. China\'s plant is 26 times bigger, and not one of these has produced a kilowatt-hour yet.'

[08:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'If you want to see what a serious long-duration wager looks like in the West, it\'s in the Mojave. Hydrostor\'s Willow Rock project in Kern County, California, cleared its final permit from the California Energy Commission in'

[09:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'December 2025. 500 MW, 4,000 MW hours. 8 hours, right in the sweet spot. California Community Power has already signed for a 50 MW slice. The design is clever. Instead of letting cavern'

[09:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pressure swing as air moves in and out, Hydrostor pushes water out of the cavern into a pond up top, and the column of water holds the pressure dead steady. Turbines hate surprises. Water as a'

[09:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'piston, it\'s absolutely awesome. Now, the timeline. Willow Rock needs 1.5 billion dollars, [music] 5 years of construction, and more than 6,000 workers. Groundbreaking is this year and Hydrostor is targeting 2030 for its'

[09:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'first test run. Hydrostor\'s Vin Walleghem would rather you look at the other end of that timeline. He says his plants last 50 years with no cost degradation. A lithium installation needs replacing after 10 to 15 years. So, the best long'

[09:50] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'duration answer in the West is correctly sized, but half a decade away from delivering a single kilowatt hour of power. China\'s plant is running right now. So, back to the thing that bugged me. If'

[10:04] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the entire advantage of compressed [music] air is cheap hours of energy storage, why would you build the world\'s biggest one and stop at 4 hours? The honest answer is that duration is only one of the main reasons to build these. China\'s grid has a specific enormous'

[10:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'problem. Just like trying to shift solar power to when it\'s needed, China\'s wind power produces cheap power in the middle of the day when nobody wants it. Then, everybody gets home at dinner time and wants it all at once. That\'s a how fast it pours problem and it has to be solved'

[10:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'at gigawatt scale night after night. For that job, what matters is megawatts and a machine that still will be running decades from now. Huai\'an gives them that and the salt geology under Jiangsu makes it cheap to do. So, Huai\'an is'

[10:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'best understood as a very large, very durable gas peaker plant replacement that happens to run on compressed air, &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; which is a perfectly good thing to build. It\'s also thinner evidence for the long duration argument than the'

[10:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'headlines make it sound. And here\'s where I have to mark my own homework. In 2024, I looked at the big nameplate megawatt number and I heard long duration. Back to the tank, I was looking at how fast it pours and calling it how much it holds. I should have done'

[11:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the division. China\'s not slowing down. Phase two of the Jintan project, also in Jiangsu, is under construction with two 350 megawatt units and 2.8 gigawatt hours. That\'s 700 megawatts into 2.8 gigawatt hours, which'

[11:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is 4 hours again. This is where the story starts losing pressure. Geology comes first. You need a salt formation, a depleted gas field,'

[11:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'or hard rock that can hold pressure for decades without leaking. And salt is a favorite because you can wash out a cavern with water and end up with walls that seal themselves. Huayan\'s caverns sit between 1,150 and 1,500 m down and'

[11:45] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hold around 980,000 cubic meters of compressed air. You either have the right rock or you don\'t. And no amount of funding changes the answer. Lithium goes anywhere on a concrete pad in 18'

[11:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'months. So any promise about where these get built, take it with a grain of salt. Also, the supply chain runs the other way from what you\'d expect. Compressed air skips lithium, cobalt, and graphite, the minerals most batteries are built'

[12:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'on. Though it still takes plenty of steel and copper. What\'s actually scarce is experience. Hardly anyone has ever bolted a 300-MW adiabatic train onto a molten salt heat store and made it work, which is why Huayan ran on Chinese-built'

[12:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'equipment start to finish. Lithium\'s problem is the materials. Air\'s problem is the machine shop. Here\'s where the two trends ran into each other, and it\'s the part [music]'

[12:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that changed my mind about whether long-duration air storage actually happens. Air is getting cheaper, and we can measure it. A peer-reviewed paper in iScience last year tracked every adiabatic compressed air project the researchers could find and fit a'

[12:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'learning curve to them. Costs fell at a 15% rate for every doubling of installed capacity between 2013 and 2024. The biggest projects, 100-MW and over, are down to roughly $120 per kWh of'

[13:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'capacity. Follow that curve and the market average drops below $157 once the world hits 10 GWh of capacity, then down to $92 past 100 GWh. That\'s measured from real projects, and'

[13:14] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the line is pointing down. Now hold that against lithium. The two cost figures don\'t line up cleanly enough to race head-to-head, so an honest comparison is a lithium against itself. 4-hour utility-scale lithium in the'

[13:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'United States runs $210 to $292 per megawatt hour without investment tax credit, or $148 to $209 with it. That\'s the number that surprised me most because it went the wrong way. Everyone'

[13:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'expected it to keep falling, but American battery storage costs are up roughly 27% since 2020. Now, most of that climb, which is 2021 through 2024, was pandemic supply chains and material prices, and the costs actually fell in'

[13:52] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '2025. The reversal is this year with rising costs again, &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; and Lazard, the bank whose annual study is the industry\'s yardstick, puts that one on tariffs and the new foreign entity rules, which have pushed the'

[14:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'supply chain through Southeast Asia at a price. For a decade, the assumption was that lithium gets cheaper every year and everything else has to chase it. In the US market, that\'s not being true. Now, I want to be careful here cuz one'

[14:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of those two numbers is a build cost, which is dollars per kilowatt hour up front, and the other is a levelized cost, but it works out to per megawatt hour over the plant\'s whole life. So, I\'m watching which way each line\'s moving, but Ares\' falling curve is'

[14:30] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'global and built almost entirely on Chinese projects. Lithium\'s rise is American and driven by trade policy. So, if the trade picture changes, it reverses. And neither project in this video sits on that curve. Vyons cost'

[14:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '$520 million for 2.4 gigawatt hours. That\'s about $217 per kilowatt hour. And Wularock works out closer to $375. Both well above the $120 the curve'

[14:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'promises, which tells you something about the distance between a learning curve and a construction site. China built 4 hours because 4 hours is what China\'s grid pays for. America may end up building 8 or 12 for a reason China'

[15:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'never had. The cheap lithium option got taken off the table. 2 years ago, I said that compressed air was finally here. Now, I\'ve revised that. The machine works. 71% is real progress from 42%. The cost curve is'

[15:19] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'pointing down even if no project in this video has actually landed on it yet. And Google and California are signing contracts, which tells me more than any press release does. Calling it a solved problem is still premature because'

[15:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'almost nothing running today actually runs long enough to earn the long duration name. The plants with the impressive name plates empty out in 4 hours. The projects with the right duration are small or under built or both. Willow Rock is a 2030 story at'

[15:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'best. Ireland is a 2028 story. So, here\'s where I land. Energy CO2 are going to probably take long duration storage. And the case is now strong enough that I put some money on it.'

[15:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Paying for the pump and the tank separately is a real structural advantage, whoever happens to be [music] saying it. And past 8 hours of energy storage, the economics aren\'t close. The hardware runs 50 years instead of 15, and it doesn\'t touch the battery mineral'

[16:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'chain at all. Lithium keeps everything up to 4 hours, and it should. It\'s better at that job, and you can build it anywhere. What I\'m less sure about is who gets there first. China has proven the machine works at gigawatt scale and'

[16:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'hasn\'t needed to build it long. The US knows exactly how long to build it and can\'t get it out of the permitting office. If that\'s still true in 2030, the failure is ours. And that record that China just set, a 4.2 gigawatt hour'

[16:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'project in central China, breaks ground this year. The world\'s largest battery has about a 2-year shelf life, which feels about right for a technology that\'s spent 50 years going nowhere [music]'

[16:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and then started moving all at once. But what do you think? Is this the storage that we\'ve been waiting for, or is it all just hot air? Let me know in the comments. You can also check out extended cuts of this video over on Patreon, where I get into why China\'s caverns and hydro storage work are'

[17:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'completely different pressure designs, and what that trade-off costs. If human-written and research videos matter to you, Patreon support helps a ton. [music] And a big welcome to new supporter plus member John Ventner. The link\'s in the description if you\'d like to join, but'

[17:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'just watching, subscribing, and hitting the notification bell is awesome. And check out my follow-up podcast Still to Be Determined. We\'ll keep this conversation going. Keep your mind open. Stay curious. And I\'ll see you in the next one.'
